In Snowflake, micro-partitions are immutable once created.

That means:

Existing micro-partitions are never updated directly
When data changes (UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE), Snowflake:
Creates new micro-partitions
Marks old ones as obsolete
Maintains metadata for Time Travel and Fail-safe

Example:

UPDATE customers
SET city = 'Richmond'
WHERE customer_id = 101;

Snowflake does NOT edit the existing partition.
Instead:

Reads old partition
Creates a new version with updated data
Keeps old partition temporarily for Time Travel

This immutability helps with:

Fast query performance
Atomic transactions
Time Travel
Zero-copy cloning
Concurrent reads/writes without locking issues
