When you perform clustering in Snowflake, Snowflake does not modify existing micro-partitions directly.

Instead, Snowflake:

Reads existing micro-partitions
Reorganizes the data based on clustering keys
Creates new optimized micro-partitions
Marks old partitions for cleanup later

So clustering still follows the immutable micro-partition architecture.

Example:

ALTER TABLE sales
CLUSTER BY (sale_date, region);

Suppose your table originally had random data distribution:

MP1	2025 + 2026 mixed
MP2	East + West mixed
MP3	All dates mixed

After clustering:

Snowflake rewrites the table internally into better-organized partitions:
New MP1	2025 Jan only
New MP2	2025 Feb only
New MP3	West region
New MP4	East region

Benefits:

Better micro-partition pruning
Less data scanned
Faster queries
Lower compute cost

Especially useful for large tables queried often with filters like:

WHERE sale_date = '2026-05-01'
AND region = 'East'

Important:

Clustering is a background maintenance process
Automatic Clustering service may continuously recluster tables
Reclustering consumes credits because Snowflake rewrites partitions
Old micro-partitions remain temporarily for:
Time Travel
Fail-safe

Think of clustering like:

“Repacking books on shelves in sorted order without editing the books themselves.”

Marks old partitions for cleanup later ? how?

When Snowflake creates new micro-partitions during operations like:

UPDATE
DELETE
MERGE
Clustering/Reclustering

…the old micro-partitions are not immediately deleted.

Instead, Snowflake marks them as:

inactive/obsolete
no longer part of the current table version

But they are still retained temporarily for:

Time Travel
Fail-safe
Running queries that started earlier

Flow:

Old Micro-Partition (Active)
        ↓
UPDATE / MERGE / CLUSTER
        ↓
New Optimized Micro-Partition Created
        ↓
Old Partition Marked Obsolete
        ↓
Retained for Time Travel
        ↓
Moved to Fail-safe
        ↓
Physically Purged

Example:

Suppose this partition exists:

MP100
customer_id 101
city = NY

You run:

UPDATE customers
SET city='Richmond'
WHERE customer_id=101;

Snowflake:

Creates new partition MP200
MP200 contains updated row
MP100 becomes obsolete

Current queries now read only MP200.

But MP100 is still stored internally.

Retention timeline:

1. Time Travel Period

Typically:

1 day (Standard Edition)
Up to 90 days (Enterprise)

During this time:

You can restore old data
Run historical queries

Example:

SELECT *
FROM customers
AT (TIMESTAMP => '2026-05-20 10:00:00');

Snowflake may read old partition MP100.

2. Fail-safe Period

After Time Travel expires:

Snowflake moves old partitions into Fail-safe
Lasts 7 days
Only Snowflake support can recover data
3. Physical Cleanup

After Fail-safe:

Snowflake garbage-collects the old micro-partitions
Storage is finally released

So “cleanup later” means:

Snowflake delays physical deletion until all recovery windows expire.
